[Music]  [Music]  today as we honor and as we learn from  Kofi Annan we take great pride in the  small role that we played in his  education and in shaping his early  career but we also bear witness to the  importance of maintaining the openness  of our universities to foreign students  scholars and faculty we must guard even  in these complicated times and protect  this fundamental American and academic  value and principle it is my privilege  and honor once again to welcome to MIT  the secretary-general of the United  Nations  His Excellency Kofi Annan  thank you then  thank you very much my dear friends  president vest let me thank you for  those very warm and kind words of  introduction Indian Amnon Zee I'm really  happy that I'm back here with all of you  as Sloan distinguished faculty members  and students and dear friends it is  indeed a pleasure for me to be back here  and to join you today for this  anniversary celebration over the course  of half a century Sloan School of  Management has built itself into one of  the world's academic powerhouses I say  world and not just country because right  from the beginning  Sloan looked well beyond the confines of  this campus encourage people from many  nations as we heard from the President  to study here and was eager to find and  advance the course of international  cooperation scholarly and otherwise  congratulations on this milestone three  decades ago I was fortunate myself to  become part of the Sloan community and  it is good to see so many familiar faces  since I've been down here yesterday and  and an event like this really brings  back memories of when I first came here  what the place was like and where I  myself was at in the state at that stage  of my life I recall in particular the  slowness posed me to some very  interesting work in organizational  culture and psychology and how to manage  change that may sound like jargon to  most of you but I can assure you that it  came in very handy at the United Nations  and I leading  back in 1982 when I was working with a  High Commissioner for Refugees in Geneva  and were trying to grapple with the  crisis of the board people I invited my  good friend and professor Cheyne to come  to Geneva to help us improve our  internal communication and to review our  mission when we gathered everybody came  we thought we're going to have a  straightforward discussion for one hour  three hours later we were all physically  and emotionally drained from what had  quickly turned out and to be a frank  soul-searching exercise for all of us  what it meant to be working for the  United Nations what were we trying to do  with the refugee crisis of the time and  how best to get different people and  people from nationalities around the  world to work better together some  frustrations had been bottled up  obviously for years but once the  floodgates were open we found new ways  forward and a truly new sense of unity  and purpose image the session was so  successful that I organized another one  in 1990 we're at shine and professor  Lester Thurow came to talk to the group  we gathered about 30 senior officials  from 26 different countries to discuss  similar issues and they all walked away  very enthusiastic I like to think that  we are replicating that exercise on a  global level among peoples and nations  as we strive to build the trust  confidence and the sense of shared  values and responsibility needed to  address the urgent issues and threats of  our time we are all aware that more and  more challenges from environmental  degradation to drug trafficking and the  spread of diseases such as hiv/aids have  a global diamond  through work travel and trips to the  store we can all see that trade and  communications are stitching human  family ever more closely together these  phenomena are also helped have also  helped to make the 21st century a very  troubling times for our global village  distrust between cultures and religion  often leads to violence and has been  aggravated by the terrorist attacks of  9/11 concern is mounting because of  global economic uncertainty and because  the benefits of globalization have been  shared so unevenly confidence in Marcus  has been dealt a feather blow with a  series of corporate scandals in the  United States and the gathering feeling  that Marcus by themselves cannot respond  to the real needs of society or provide  the Bob publicans that humankind needs  to survive in an age of interdependence  global citizenship is a crucial pillar  of progress in a series of global  meetings and conferences over the past  two years in particular world leaders  have tried to define what that  citizenship means they have been trying  to build an inclusive responsive  effective international system which all  people can bend from which all people  can benefit from and in which all feel  they have a stake shared responsibility  was at the heart of the declaration  adopted at the Millennium summit in  September 2000 160 heads of state and  government gathered in New York all  countries came together not just to  express their general hopes for peace  and development in the 21st century but  also to give their back they are back  into a set of various  time-bound objectives which have since  become known as the Millennium  Development Goals the goals include  reducing hunger provide an access to  safe drinking water and ensuring  universal primary education they will be  closely monitored and measured how many  kiss I in school how quickly hunger and  extreme poverty are being reduced and we  will advertise the resource in such a  way that we hope will galvanize politics  and policymaking so that the goals can  be met by the target date of 2015  governments face a first first test of  their commitment to these goals last  November at the World Trade Organization  meeting in Doha their trust was the main  issue on the table trust because the  development countries have had a lot of  talk about free and fair trade but seen  too little far too little of it they  want to know that they approach their  products will have an equal chance to  compete in the global market that  chances that chance is currently denied  them because of tariffs on their goods  and because of subsidies given to their  competitors in the rich countries  subsidies that I might add also  perpetuate unsustainable practices in  farming transport and energy use the new  round of negotiations agree to adorn her  office the prospects that Marcus will  truly be open but it is only a promise  and it is too soon to say whether that  trust in the trading system will be  achieved only time will tell but I think  we need to work hard and press ahead to  open the markets  truly for all countries  next came the Monterey conference on  financing for development last March  that conference was also an exercise in  recognizing shared responsibilities the  conference generated substantial new  pledges for official development  assistance reversing a decade-long  decline and made good progress on issues  such as debt relief investment and  corruption just as as important  developed and development countries  reached a common understanding on their  respective responsibilities in the  pursuit of balanced and equitable  development and finally last month our  the World Summit on sustainable  development in Johannesburg global  citizenship once again took center stage  all leaders committed themselves to a  path of development and economic growth  that saved gas resources and ecosystems  for succeeding generations leaders from  rich countries in particular agreed to  reduce their nation's ecological  footprint on the planet taken together  these summits and conferences give us a  blueprint a blueprint for development  that puts people not States not GDP  statistics at the center of policymaking  the overriding challenge now is  implementation and for that we shall  need people from all sectors the public  sector private sector civil society to  forge more and better partnerships to  implement these schemes one of the most  welcomed developments are the United  Nations in recent years has been the  steadily growing engagement of the  business community both in policy forums  and in projects on the ground though the  relationship is not without its  difficulties there is growing  recognition that we must move beyond  politics of confrontation and the  solutions to poverty environmental  degradation and other challenges can  only be found if the private sector is  involved and is an effective partner  more and more businesses themselves are  coming to recognize how they depend on  international norms and standards for  the conduct of business on a global  scale and that the United Nations  wide-ranging work in peace and  development has an impact on how they do  in 1999 at the World Economic Forum in  Davos I introduced the idea of a Global  Compact the Global Compact was based on  my belief that Marcus and human  wellbeing can go hand in hand over the  long run human wellbeing can be  dramatically advanced by  well-functioning Marcus but Marcus  themselves cannot be sustained if they  do not ensure human well-being as I  asked businesses at that time to embrace  nine universal principles in the areas  of Human Rights core labor standards and  the environment and to enact these  principles within their sphere of  influence and make it part of the way of  doing business I picked these ideas  because I was worried by a severe  imbalance in global rulemaking while  there are extensive and enforceable  rules for economic priorities such as  intellectual property rights there are  few strong measures for equally vital  concerns such as human rights and the  environment  the compact has since become more than a  call to action today it involves not  only businesses but also labor  Federation's and non-governmental  organizations it has promoted the  importance of unity  values and encourage investors to look  harder at opportunities in the least  developed countries particularly in  Africa the compact has created the Lenin  forum a worldwide economy academic  network that examines case studies  trying to determine what works and what  doesn't and I'm pleased that Sloan plays  an important role in this forum none of  this is meant to is meant as a  substitute for action by governments or  as a regulatory framework or code of  conduct rather the compact is a  voluntary initiative a platform for  showing how markets can be made to serve  the needs of society as a whole business  may ask why they should go down the spot  especially if it involves taking steps  that the competition might not or steps  they feel are rightly the province of  governments sometimes doing what is  right for example a Curie efficiency or  creating decent decent workplace  conditions is in the immediate interest  of business sometimes we must do what is  right simply because not to do so would  be wrong and sometimes we do what is  right to help usher in a new day of new  reforms and new behaviors we do not want  business to do anything different from  the UH normal business we want business  to do their business differently we want  them to continue what they do but bear  in mind these values that touch all of  us dear friends openness is the Imagine  hallmark of our time but we need to make  it work otherwise countries and peoples  might  retreat behind protectionism or worst of  all reject global citizenship or  globalization in favor of narrow  concepts of national interest not at all  appropriate for this interdependent  world we live in for this global village  business is well-placed not only to  generate employment investment and  growth but also to advance the global  citizenship Sloane is well-placed to  teach more than economics accountant and  finance and to help define the  parameters of corporate citizenship and  I am delighted to hear that your program  is already evolving in that direction  and there's a great deal of enthusiasm  for the program on corporate citizenship  and the United Nations is well-placed to  promote dialogue that were build trust  and create the multilateral norms and  frameworks needed to fulfill our shared  responsibilities all of us the private  sector civil society labor unions NGOs  universities and foundations and  individuals like you must come together  in an alliance for progress together we  can and must move from value to values  from shareholders to stakeholders and  from balance sheets to balanced  development together we can and must  face the dangers ahead and bring  solutions Within Reach  thank you very much and congratulations  on this to the Sloan community on this  important day I will now be happy to  take your questions  [Applause]  Thank You mr. secretary-general  I have a number of questions from the  audience and perhaps more will be  forthcoming but let me let me pose one  for you do you see a link between  corporate responsibility and corporate  citizenship on the one hand and the gulf  between haves and have-nots on the other  do companies have a positive role to  play in creating a sustainable future I  think from what I have said this morning  it is clear that I see a very important  role for the corporate world and I think  corporations that respond and react to  these norms do make a difference in the  communities and the societies in which  they operate corporations and the  leaders of corporations are often very  important personalities in their own  communities and can influence policy and  decision making by governments and help  steer laws in the right direction but  they also make a difference in the  day-to-day lives of the of the  individual corporations don't have to  wait for governments to pass laws for  them to know that their productions  should not pollute the water that  produces fish for the village they need  to they need not wait for governments to  pay a decent wage they need not wait for  governments to try and train their  people and give them a take the question  of hiv/aids corporations have done  fantastic things around the world  Volkswagen do Brasil for years watched  is good managers contract the disease  and die off and they took the decision  we are going to do something about this  came up with a program of education and  treatment for their managers and the arc  families and then begin to see these  creative men and women live on continue  to deliver today  and passing the message on to their  families and communities how to protect  yourself so the role of corporations in  issue of development and their society  is extremely important and some are  doing extremely well now at edge or this  to look at what works and what doesn't  and corporations do have a role thank  you another question from the audience  is what can we at MIT at Sloan and those  of us assembled do in particular to help  realize or advance the Global Compact  initiative  I think you've you're already on the  right track by training your students  and discussing the Global Compact  initiative with them and I'm sure you  also have a chance to advise many  corporations and you would be able to  expose them to this and talk to them  about it I've I've had a chance of  meeting some of the Sloan fellows in New  York and I've been struck by how  enthusiastic they are and I know that if  a quite a few of your professors are  also working on the Global Compact and  so I think apart from IDI teaching it  here but the learning forum and what we  are going to put on the web to share  with others becomes an important way of  spreading the word the other thing that  we are doing is a learning forums that  we've introduced where for example  corporations can come together to  discuss what their responsibilities are  and what their posture should be if they  find themselves operating in areas of  conflict  what is suspected of corporations whom  should they tend to for advice and how  did they work together to cope and all  this I think is going to be extremely  helpful for corporations and I'm  extremely happy that US corporations are  beginning to join the compact we've had  tremendous response from Europe from  Latin America Asia and elsewhere in  Africa recently Hewitt pocket has joined  FISA is coming on board and I expect  many more will  what responsibilities do American  corporations in particular have in the  global community  you see us you see a special  responsibility because of well I think  today it is it is it is business and  these corporations that have become the  main motor for creative for the creation  of wealth they have technology they have  money and they have management and they  also have a global rich in some ways a  corporation's understood faster and  better than governments that we do live  in a global world and they work across  borders and they also know that in doing  that you need to have some rules and I  think the corporations that operate  around the world should see their role  not only I say New York headquartered or  Silicon Valley headquartered but they  should try and also lift the people in  the countries in which they are  operating and also help the societies by  passing on some of the knowledge and  some of the technology and the training  where they fail to do this they  inadvertently become part of the  conflict or the problem we've seen as  can give you an example look at what is  happening in Nigeria with Shell BP Shell  where the villages around the oil  production sites feel they have seen  nothing of the benefits that accrue from  exploitation of the oil the money goes  to the central government the probe the  shareholders take their profits and they  see nothing and continue to live in  squalor I think it was two or three  months ago when a group of women  demonstrated and took over one of the  side now shell has indicated it is going  to help them and is going to put some  money back and in fact even if it is not  direct responsibility of shell in a way  could have pressure the government said  don't you think we should be doing  something for the villages around here  can we pull together and do something  these kinds of gestures go a long way  and I know some companies have done it  an average or they said to do that not  only train the local people pass on  technology but ensure that we spread the  benefits a little bit more I have a one  word question which I think we'd all be  pleased if you would address briefly and  that question is Iraq I thought I was  going to get away with not a perfect  screen yeah no Iraq obviously is a very  topical issue is a very difficult issue  for all of us and the UN has been  involved with this problem for over 12  years now and the inspectors as you know  we're we're drawn from Iraq in December  98 and since then we've not had  inspectors back in Iraq earlier this  year I got a letter from the Secretary  General of the Arab League saying the  Iraqis would want to sit with you to  discuss the return of inspectors without  conditions so we arranged to meet and  that was I think in February or March we  we met three times as here the last  meeting was in Vienna we seem to be  inching forward but no real breakthrough  or progress and then the General  Assembly met on the 12th of September  and at that time the US government had  become very focused on the Iraqi issue I  I spoke and President Bush spoke we both  demanded Iraq to comply in my statement  I made it  several points but the two I would want  to share with you argue in that any  country when attacked has an inherent  right of self-defense under article 51  of the UN Charter but when the country  decides to deal with a broader threat to  international peace and security the  approval and support of the Security  Council is required that we need to have  that basic understanding otherwise we  will face major difficulties down the  line so we were all delighted when  President Bush spoke and informed the  world that he's going to refer the Iraqi  issue to the United Nations I also  indicated to the council that in such  situations they will also have to have  the will and live up to their  responsibilities the Security Council  you know what has happened in the  Congress and so I'm not going to come  and comment on that but in the Security  Council there is a debate going on now  as to the second stances under which the  inspector should be sent back there is a  sense that the disarmament regime will  need to be strengthened the inspectors  would need to be strengthened so that we  don't repeat the errors of the past and  that they could go in and they can go in  with a strengthened hand and get their  work done my sense is that that  resolution will be passed there so lots  of discussions and negotiations going on  and I expect the council to send in the  inspectors with their strengthened  regime with a demand that Iran should  perform and with the request that the  inspector should report back should Iraq  fail to do so and if Iraq wait have  failed to do so  I suspect the council will indicate the  consequences that Iraq will have to face  up to there has been a suggestion that  maybe in even in this resolution they  should be the phrase if Iraq fails all  necessary means must be used which is a  code word for me  three could phrase for military action  when I think the member-states wanted to  stage approach sending the inspectors if  they get into trouble if it fails come  back and we'll pass the second  resolution so this is where we are and I  expect that by within the next week or  two the council would have acted the  chief inspector Hans Blix and his team  are ready to go they met with the Iraqis  and discussed the practical aspects of  resumption of their work but they are  waiting for the council to act so that  if there are new instructions they can  factor it into their work and of course  all the Member States will work together  and my plea to the council is that they  should work in unity from experience it  is only when the council is united that  they have the greatest impact and I hope  and trust that the council will be able  to manage this process in the way that  we keep the unity of their member states  together we keep the USA in the fold  working with the Security Council not  just for today but for tomorrow and the  day after thank you very much  Thank You mr. secretary  you

